"""
`width: auto` on an absolutely positioned box is SHRINK-TO-FIT, not "fill the
containing block".

`absolute_outer_width` had three arms — both-offsets-given (stretch), a
percentage width, and an explicit width — and then fell through to
`constrained_outer_width(node, st, containing_w)`. That fallback made every
`width:auto` absolutely positioned box as wide as its containing block, which
CSS 2.1 10.3.7 reserves for the case where BOTH `left` and `right` are given
(the first arm already handles that one).

The damage was not confined to the width. `absolute_child_x` resolves a `right`
offset as `parent_x + border + padding_box_w - OUTER_WIDTH - right`, so a width
that is too large by 733 px drags x left by the same amount. On the catalog's
one absolutely positioned element this was measured as:

    Chrome   x=769 y=227 w=77 h=32
    Simple   x=35  y=227 w=810 h=32     (dx 734, dw 733, dy 0, dh 0)

dy and dh being exactly 0 is the diagnostic that mattered: `top: 10px` and the
height were already right, so this was never "absolute positioning is
unimplemented" — it was one missing arm in the width resolution. That single
row carried Sigma 1467, which was 99% of `css-layout`'s error and 26% of the
whole 8-page catalog's.

Every number below is HARVESTED from Chrome 152 `--headless=new
--window-size=900,20000` on the fixture this spec feeds the engine, not
predicted:

| case                                   | Chrome x,y,w,h  |
|----------------------------------------|-----------------|
| `right:10;top:10`, auto width          | 814,10,77,32    |
| `left:10`, auto width                  | 10,80,77,32     |
| explicit `width:300px`                 | 590,150,300,32  |
| BOTH `left:10` and `right:10`          | 10,220,880,32   |
| auto width, content wider than the block | 0,290,890,56  |
| a STATIC span in the same block        | 133,353,37,18   |

The fifth row is the one that fixed the clamp bound. Shrink-to-fit is
`min(max(preferred-minimum, available), preferred)`, and `available` is the
containing block MINUS whichever offset is specified — Chrome clamps an
overflowing box to 890 in a 900 px block, not to 900, because the `right: 10px`
is already committed. Clamping to the full containing block would have been
invisible on the catalog (whose one absolute box does not overflow) and wrong
everywhere else, so it was measured rather than assumed.

TWO RESIDUES, stated rather than absorbed into a fitted constant:

1. AC-1 lands **76 against Chrome's 77** — the same class of integer
   text-advance residue `form_control_intrinsic_width_spec` records for
   `<textarea cols=40>`. It is below the geometry differ's 1 px tolerance, which
   is why the catalog row clears completely, and it is NOT papered over by
   adding 1 to the intercept: the shrink-to-fit width is a max-content
   measurement, and bending it here would mis-size every other box that uses the
   same measurement. Note AC-1b: x comes out at Chrome's 814 EXACTLY, because
   the `right` formula subtracts the width from the right edge and the residue
   lands on the left edge, which is off-screen of the comparison.
2. `right: auto` is NOT honoured. Written as
   `style="right:auto;left:10px"` over a class that sets `right: 10px`, the
   engine returns 890 (the `available` clamp) where Chrome returns 77. AC-2
   therefore uses a box that never sets `right` at all, which exercises the same
   shrink-to-fit path without the `auto` keyword. This is a SEPARATE defect in
   offset-keyword parsing, not in the width arm this spec covers, and it is
   filed rather than fixed here —
   `doc/08_tracking/bug/absolute_right_auto_not_honoured_2026-09-14.md`.

SABOTAGE (run before landing, each with `grep -c SABOTAGED` confirming the edit
actually applied):

* restoring the old fallback `constrained_outer_width(node, st, containing_w)`
  fails AC-1/AC-2/AC-5 — AC-1 returns to the measured defect (x=35, w=810) —
  while AC-3 (explicit width) and AC-4 (both offsets) stay green. That is the
  proof this arm is the one that was missing and that it did not swallow the
  arms above it.
* clamping to `padding_box_w` instead of `available` fails ONLY AC-5, at 900
  against Chrome's 890, with AC-1/2/3/4 green. That separates the clamp BOUND
  from the shrink-to-fit rule itself: one shared bound could not have fitted
  both the fitting and the overflowing case.

The controls are the other half. AC-3 and AC-4 pin that the two arms ABOVE the
new one still win, AC-6 pins that a static sibling in the same mixed-content
block is untouched, and AC-7 pins the container itself — an absolute child is
out of flow and must not change its parent's box.
"""

"""
Traceability: REQ-BROWSER-ENGINE-LAYOUT-014 — an absolutely positioned box with `width: auto` resolves its width by shrink-to-fit against the containing block minus any specified offset, and only stretches when both `left` and `right` are given.
"""
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/browser_engine/simple_web_html_layout_renderer_layout.spl 1%
use std.spec.*
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.browser_engine.simple_web_html_layout_renderer.{
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id
}

val VW: i32 = 900
val VH: i32 = 20000
val HEAD: text = "<html><head><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;font:16px/1.5 sans-serif}.positioned{position:relative;min-height:70px;background:#e0f2fe}.badge{position:absolute;right:10px;top:10px;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:999px;color:white;background:#047857}</style></head><body>"

fn probe(inner: text, id: text, prop: text) -> text:
    simple_web_layout_debug_layout_by_id(HEAD + inner + "</body></html>", VW, VH, id, prop)

# The catalog's own shape: a relative container with mixed content (text plus an
# absolutely positioned span).
val CATALOG: text = "<div class=\"positioned\" id=\"c\">Relative container <span class=\"badge\" id=\"a\">absolute</span></div>"

describe "width:auto on an absolutely positioned box is shrink-to-fit":
    it "AC-1: right:10 with auto width takes the content's width, not the block's":
        step("Chrome 77; the pre-fix engine returned 810, the whole containing block")
        step("STATED: 76, one px under Chrome — the text-advance residue, see the header")
        expect(probe(CATALOG, "a", "w")).to_equal("76")

    it "AC-1b: and the right offset then resolves x to Chrome's value exactly":
        step("Chrome harvests 814; the wrong width had dragged this to 35")
        expect(probe(CATALOG, "a", "x")).to_equal("814")

    it "AC-2: a left offset with auto width shrink-to-fits to the same width":
        step("which side is specified changes x, not the shrink-to-fit width")
        step("Chrome harvests 10,10,77,32 for this box; same 1 px advance residue")
        expect(probe("<div class=\"positioned\">Relative container <span id=\"a\" style=\"position:absolute;left:10px;top:10px;padding:4px 8px\">absolute</span></div>", "a", "w")).to_equal("76")

    it "AC-3 (control): an explicit width still wins over shrink-to-fit":
        step("the explicit-width arm sits above the new one and must keep winning")
        expect(probe("<div class=\"positioned\">Relative container <span class=\"badge\" id=\"a\" style=\"width:300px\">absolute</span></div>", "a", "w")).to_equal("300")

    it "AC-4 (control): BOTH left and right still STRETCH, per 10.3.7":
        step("Chrome 880 = 900 - 10 - 10; this is the one case that does fill the block")
        expect(probe("<div class=\"positioned\">Relative container <span class=\"badge\" id=\"a\" style=\"left:10px\">absolute</span></div>", "a", "w")).to_equal("880")

    it "AC-5: content wider than the block clamps to the block MINUS the offset":
        step("Chrome 890 in a 900 px block, because right:10px is already committed")
        expect(probe("<div class=\"positioned\">Relative container <span class=\"badge\" id=\"a\">absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute absolute</span></div>", "a", "w")).to_equal("890")

    it "AC-6 (control): a STATIC span in the same mixed block is untouched":
        step("the change is confined to the absolute arm; an inline sibling keeps its advance")
        expect(probe("<div class=\"positioned\">Relative container <span id=\"a\">static</span></div>", "a", "w")).to_equal("37")

    it "AC-7 (control): the container's own box does not move":
        step("an absolutely positioned child is out of flow and cannot resize its parent")
        expect(probe(CATALOG, "c", "w")).to_equal("900")

    it "AC-8 (control): nor does the container's height":
        step("min-height:70px still governs; Chrome 70")
        expect(probe(CATALOG, "c", "h")).to_equal("70")
